rent accessibility tree, I can see that under the 'main' role, there are links that are clearly issue titles, such as '404 for many URLs', 'Checklist enhancements', etc., each with URLs containing '/-/issues/'. The 'Open 41' tab is selected, confirming these are open issues. The issues are sorted by 'Created date' in 'Descending' order, so the first ones listed are the most recent. To complete the task, I need to extract these issue titles correctly. The previous code attempted to filter by href containing '/-/issues/', but it included links like 'New issue' (which has a different URL pattern) and numeric counts (which are links to notes, not issue titles). I need to refine the selection to only include links that are actual issue titles. From the tree, each issue title link is directly under 'main' and has a URL pattern like '/a11yproject/a11yproject.com/-/issues/611'. I can use page.get_by_role('link') under 'main' and filter by the href attribute to ensure it matches this pattern exactly. Additionally, I should exclude links that are not issue titles, such as 'New issue', 'Bulk edit', assignee links, label links, and numeric note counts. The numeric note counts have URLs ending with '#notes', so I can filter those out. I'll write code to get all links under 'main', filter for those with href containing '/-/issues/' and not ending with '#notes', and then collect their text content. This should yield only the issue titles. I don't need to use any knowledge base functions because none are relevant to extracting issues from the current page. The functions provided are for navigation, but I'm already on the issues page. After extracting the titles, I will terminate with the result as a list of the most recent open issue titles.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Get all links under the main role\n    all_links = page.get_by_role('main').get_by_role('link')\n    # Wait for at least one link to be present\n    await all_links.first.wait_for(state='attached')\n    \n    issue_titles = []\n    count = await all_links.count()\n    for i in range(count):\n        link = all_links.nth(i)\n        # Get the href attribute to check if it's an issue title link\n        href = await link.get_attribute('href')\n        if href and '/-/issues/' in href and not href.endswith('#notes'):\n            # Get the text content of the link\n            title = await link.text_content()\n            if title and title.strip() not in ['New issue', 'Bulk edit']:  # Exclude non-issue links\n                issue_titles.append(title.strip())\n    \n    # Return the list of issue titles\n    return issue_titles\n</code>\n\n<result>\n['404 for many URLs', 'Checklist enhancements', 'Audit redesign spacing sizes and turn them into variables', 'Set up environment variables', 'Add functionality to expand/collapse sections of the checklist, or the entire checklist page', 'Explore less thin secondary font options', 'Remove Gulp as a dependency', 'Create an Offline page', 'Articles wanted: auditing the accessibiltiy of iOS and Android mobile apps', 'Add the level of the rule directly on the item listed', 'Article: CSS layouts and its impact on accessibility.', 'Content: Checklist WCAG references should be changed for better accuracy', 'Non-Github contribution guidelines need clarification', 'We should provide a callout card component for emphasizing content', 'We should provide syntactic sugar for creating figures in Markdown', 'Article: Starting a design with accessibility', 'Our non-GitHub editorial process is opaque', 'The process for writing for us is both scattered and buried', 'In error state, focused controls take on purple or red-and-purple focus indicator', 'Investigate if <details>/<summary> is a good choice for our Checklist']\n</result>\n</previous_actions>\n\nHere is the current website state:\n<webpage_accessibility_tree>\ndocument: \"Issues \u00b7 The A11Y Project / a11yproject.com \u00b7 GitLab\" {\n  link: \"Skip to content\", u